# Manager Dashboard 7a — Scaffold, Auth, API Client, Live Map

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** First half of PLAN.md 7.1/7.2 — a Vite+React+TS+Tailwind app with login, a typed API client, and a live map of rep positions polling `GET /api/v1/pings/latest`. Second half (zone drawing, task/goal admin, violations feed, push opt-in — PLAN.md 7.3/7.4) is a separate plan, `2026-07-20-dashboard-7b-*.md`, written after this one is verified working, mirroring the visits/tasks (4a/4b) split for size.

**Why this phase is verified differently than every backend phase so far:** `docs/design.md`'s own CI bar for the dashboard is "typecheck+build," not "test" (PLAN.md 9.2) — a lower bar than the backend's, deliberately. `tsc --noEmit` and `vite build` prove the code compiles; they do not prove a Leaflet map actually renders tiles, a marker actually appears, or a login form actually logs in. A text-only subagent reviewer reading a diff cannot see a rendered map either. So: implementer + reviewer pairs still run per task (spec compliance + code quality, same rigor as every backend phase), but the pass/fail gate per task is **typecheck + build succeeds**, plus a small number of **targeted unit tests for the pieces that have real logic** (the auth-refresh-retry interceptor, the polling hook) — not exhaustive component tests, which the design's own stated bar doesn't ask for and which would be gold-plating. At the end of this plan, the controller (not a subagent) runs the app in a real browser via the `/browse` skill and visually confirms: login works, the map renders tiles, at least one rep marker appears. This is the step that actually proves the feature works, and it happens once, after all tasks, not per-task.

**Architecture:** New `dashboard/` directory at the repo root, sibling to `backend/`. Vite + React 18 + TypeScript (strict) + Tailwind CSS v4 (via `@tailwindcss/vite`, no separate PostCSS config needed). `react-router-dom` for routing. `react-leaflet` + raw `leaflet` for the map (no `react-leaflet-draw`-style wrapper package — that's Phase 7b's problem and gets decided there). No ESLint/Prettier added — `tsc --noEmit` is the correctness gate this repo's own plan calls for; adding a second linting tool isn't requested and isn't free (config, CI time, another thing to keep green).

## Global Constraints

- **Auth contract, verified against the real backend before writing this plan**: `X-Tenant-Id` header is required ONLY on `POST /api/v1/auth/login` (and `/register`, unused by this dashboard) — every other authenticated request uses `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>` alone; the backend derives `tenant_id` server-side from the token's subject. Do not send `X-Tenant-Id` on any call except login. Getting this backwards (e.g. sending it everywhere, or never sending it) breaks either login or every other call.
- **Login is the only auth flow this dashboard needs.** No self-serve registration UI — nothing in this codebase has a tenant-creation/onboarding concept yet (tenant IDs are provisioned out-of-band), and PLAN.md 7.1 only asks for "auth," not "sign up." The login form takes a raw tenant ID (UUID) as a text field — clunky, but it's the actual backend contract as built; resolving a company name to a tenant ID is a real feature nothing has asked for. Note this as a known v1 limitation, not a bug to silently "improve" by inventing a backend endpoint.
- **Token storage**: `refresh_token` in `localStorage` (survives reloads); `access_token` in memory only (React context state, lost on reload, re-obtained via `/auth/refresh` on app mount if a refresh token exists). This is a deliberate simplification given the backend's existing bearer-token-in-JSON-body contract (not httpOnly cookies) — acceptable for an internal manager tool, not a public consumer app. Don't invent a cookie-based flow; that would require backend changes out of scope here.
- **After login, reject non-manager accounts at the UI layer** — if `/auth/me` returns `role !== "manager"`, show an error ("this dashboard is for managers") and don't persist the session, even though the backend would technically issue the rep a valid token. Nearly everything this dashboard calls is manager-only anyway; failing fast with one clear message beats a rep hitting silent 403s on every screen.
- **The API client's 401 handling**: on any 401 response, attempt exactly ONE `/auth/refresh` call using the stored refresh token, then retry the ORIGINAL request exactly once. If the refresh call itself fails (expired/invalid refresh token), clear storage and force a redirect to `/login` — do not loop, do not retry more than once.
- **Leaflet has two classic bundler gotchas that compile clean and fail silently in the browser** — get both right the first time, since neither shows up in `tsc`/`vite build`, only in a real page load:
  1. Leaflet's CSS (`leaflet/dist/leaflet.css`) must be imported somewhere in the app (e.g. `main.tsx`) or the map renders as a blank/broken grid of tile fragments with no layout.
  2. Leaflet's default marker icon images resolve via relative paths that Vite's bundler breaks, so markers render as broken image icons unless you re-point them — the standard, minimal fix is deleting `L.Icon.Default.prototype._getIconUrl` and calling `L.Icon.Default.mergeOptions({...})` with explicit imported icon URLs. Do this once in a small `src/leafletIconFix.ts` module imported at app startup, not per-component.
- **Polling interval: 20 seconds**, a concrete middle-of-the-road choice within design.md's "polls on an interval" (15-60s ping-posting cadence on the tablet side; the dashboard doesn't need to poll faster than reps report). Implement as a named constant (`MAP_POLL_INTERVAL_MS`), not a magic number.
- **No component snapshot/render tests.** Two pieces of this phase have real logic worth a unit test: the API client's 401-refresh-retry-once behavior, and the polling hook's interval-fire-and-cleanup behavior. Everything else (JSX layout, form markup) is verified by `tsc --noEmit` + `vite build` + the controller's end-of-plan browser check — writing component tests for static markup would be gold-plating relative to what this phase's own stated bar asks for.
- Directory/package naming: the app lives at `dashboard/`, uses `npm` (no reason to introduce pnpm/yarn as a second package manager alongside `uv` on the backend side).

